cartridge "NIGHTHAWK 128/CHARLES ORCUTT/1989"
' NIGHTHAWK 128 - Charles Orcutt, Commodore 128 (machine code, 80-column VDC
' bitmap), RUN magazine's ReRUN Fun Pak 128 (1989), side B.
' ColecoVision port in Amy. The rules are the C128 machine code's loop
' ($20FB-$267A, orig/nh.dis), step for step, in the C128's own units: x in
' VDC pixels 0-639, field rows 0-90, the rider's column Px 0-76. One call of
' StepRules = one pass of the C128's loop. work/nh/model.mjs is the same
' code in JavaScript, checked against VICE x128 traces (6,440 of 6,440
' loops agree); bot.mjs follows the cartridge with it.
'
' The game: you ride a scooter down a road that bends one way, then the
' other. Vampire bats come flying out of the vanishing point toward you.
' Shoot them (one shot at a time); a bat that reaches you, or riding on the
' road's edge, drains strength. Every 10 bats the level goes up (+1
' strength): bats are worth the level, and a bite costs the level.
' Strength below zero: game over.
'
' Timing: the C128 makes 3.46 loops a second (it redraws the whole 80-column
' field each pass). The port steps the rules every 17 frames (3.53 a
' second) and moves the sprites smoothly between two steps toward where the
' rules will put them next (the bats' and the shot's next places do not
' depend on the stick). The stick and the button are latched between steps.
'
' Layout: HUD row 1 (score, hi score, level), sky rows 2-9, the field rows
' 10-21 (a bitmap of unique tiles, rebuilt after each step in 12 frames,
' one tile row a frame), strength pips row 22.

